{
  "name": "Shipment Exception Watch",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## What this does\n\nEvery weekday at 8am it takes the orders that are paid but not yet delivered,\nasks the courier where each one is, and **compares that against what it wrote\ndown yesterday**.\n\nEleven orders are pinned here and they end in five different places.\n\nThree are moving normally and are never mentioned again. Three are stuck. One\nwas invoiced at nearly three times the quoted rate. Two are ambiguous, so a\nperson looks rather than an alarm firing. And two are orders the courier\nreported on yesterday and returned **nothing at all** for this morning.\n\nOne message at 8am. Not eleven alerts.",
        "height": 380,
        "width": 440,
        "color": 4
      },
      "id": "b7000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
      "name": "Note 1",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        -2500,
        -760
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Nothing announces itself\n\nThis is the sentence to open the call with, because it is what makes shipment\nmonitoring different from everything else on this canvas.\n\n**A late parcel does not raise an error and does not send a message.** It simply\nstops generating scans. The first notification anybody gets is the customer\nasking where their coat is, and by then it has been sitting in a customs shed\nfor a week.\n\nSo this workflow cannot wait to be told. It has to notice an **absence**, and\nnoticing an absence means knowing what yesterday looked like.\n\nThat is what `Write Down Today's Position` is for. Every order passes through\nit, whether or not it is a problem, and tomorrow morning's run reads it back.\n**An order that appeared yesterday and is still here is a worse problem than one\nthat appeared today**, and the brief says which is which - AL-10361 has been\nheld by US customs for five mornings running.\n\nWithout that stored line there is no such thing as \"stopped moving\". There is\nonly a status, and a status looks the same on day one and day nine.",
        "height": 480,
        "width": 480,
        "color": 3
      },
      "id": "b7000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002",
      "name": "Note 2",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        -2020,
        -760
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Two rules, and neither of them is a model\n\n**Stuck is arithmetic.** Paid more than the warehouse's own handover window ago\nand never handed over; or no scan for four days; or three days past the courier's\nown service standard for that lane. Subtraction, with the same answer twice.\n\n**The cost check is arithmetic too.** The 3PL quotes a rate per zone and weight\nbefore the parcel ships, and the courier invoices afterwards. Divide one by the\nother. AL-10419 comes back at 2.84 - a boxed coat re-weighed on volume rather\nthan actual weight, which is the commonest way shipping costs quietly leak.\n\nAsk a model whether GBP 31.80 is reasonable for a 0.8kg parcel to Warrington and\nit will answer confidently either way. It has no idea what was quoted.\n\n**The model here writes the message and nothing else.** It never sees the healthy\norders, it never decides a lane, and every figure it is given has already been\nworked out. The lane where the courier feed went silent does not reach it at\nall - that one goes straight to operations, because the right response to \"we\nhave no idea where six parcels are\" is not a well-written paragraph.\n\n**Everything that sends or writes is greyed out.** It runs end to end and leaves\nnothing behind.",
        "height": 480,
        "width": 500,
        "color": 6
      },
      "id": "b7000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003",
      "name": "Note 3",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        -1520,
        -760
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## The third outcome is the one that keeps it alive\n\nA shipment that has gone quiet is usually **ambiguous, not broken**. A long-haul\nleg to Australia is silent for three days by design. A weekend adds two.\n\nA monitor with only two answers - fine, or alarm - cries wolf inside a week, and\na team that has learned to ignore the 8am message is worse than no message at\nall.\n\nSo AL-10402 and AL-10369 go into a **\"someone should look\"** list rather than an\nalert. Same brief, different sentence, no siren.",
        "height": 320,
        "width": 420,
        "color": 5
      },
      "id": "b7000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000004",
      "name": "Note 4",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        -1000,
        -760
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "rule": {
          "interval": [
            {
              "field": "cronExpression",
              "expression": "0 8 * * 1-5"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "id": "b7000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005",
      "name": "Every Weekday, 8am",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.scheduleTrigger",
      "typeVersion": 1.2,
      "position": [
        -2480,
        200
      ]
    },
